{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1359 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "011db5b74686d0c7d3544cb00664fe1bdadc9a52a0dbda31e35efc6994aeb6bc" :edb/db-before-t 1358 :edb/db-after-t 1359 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534671 50 #inst "2026-10-12T06:59:22.719Z" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186171873 1071 "Compute $\\textrm E[5X-2Y]$ if $\\textrm E[X]=1$ and $\\textrm E[Y] = 2$." 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186171873 1071 "Compute $\\textrm E[5X-2Y]$ if $\\textrm E[X]=1$ and $\\textrm E[Y] = 2.$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186171873 1074 "For any random variables $X,Y$ and any constants $a,b$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[aX+bY] = a \\textrm E[X] + b \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X - 2Y] &= 5E[X] - 2E[Y] \\\\\n&= 5(1) - 2(2) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186171873 1074 "For any random variables $X,Y$ and any constants $a,b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[aX+bY] = a \\textrm E[X] + b \\textrm E[Y].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-2Y] & =5\\text{E}[X]-2\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =5(1)-2(2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186171874 1071 "The probability distributions of the independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nFind $\\textrm E[XY].$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186171874 1071 "The probability distributions of the independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n| $x$$-4$$-2$$2$$4$$f(x)$$\\dfrac{1}{6}$$\\dfrac{1}{3}$$\\dfrac{1}{3}$$\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |  | $y$$1$$2$$3$$f(y)$$\\dfrac{1}{4}$$\\dfrac{1}{4}$$\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\nFind $\\textrm E[XY]$." 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186171874 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are *independent* random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X\\cdot Y]=\\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= -4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = 0 \\\\\nE[Y] &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X\\cdot Y] = \\textrm E[X] \\cdot \\textrm E[Y] = 0 \\cdot \\dfrac{9}{4} = 0\n$$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186171874 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are *independent* random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X\\cdot Y]=\\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y].\n$$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=0 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X\\cdot Y] = \\textrm E[X] \\cdot \\textrm E[Y] = 0  \\cdot \\dfrac{9}{4} = 0.\n$$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186171875 1071 "Andrea has two dice. One of them is fair, and the other is not. The rolls of the unfair die follow the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected sum of the outcomes of $2$ throws of the fair die and $4$ throws of the unfair one?\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ |" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186171875 1071 "Andrea has two dice. One of them is fair, and the other is not. The rolls of the unfair die follow the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected sum of the outcomes of $2$ throws of the fair die and $4$ throws of the unfair one?\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ |" 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186171875 1074 "Let $X_i$ be the $i$th score obtained from the fair die, and let $Y_j$ be the $j$th score obtained from the unfair die. Also, let $X$ and $Y$ represent the scores obtained from a typical throw of the fair and unfair die, respectively.\n\nThen, we need to calculate\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^2 X_i + \\sum_{j=1}^4 Y_j \\right]\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[∑_(i = 1)^(2)X_{i} + ∑_(j = 1)^(4)Y_{j}] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(2)E[X_{i}] + ∑_(j = 1)^(4)E[Y_{j}] \\\\\n&= 2E[X] + 4E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the last equality above is true because\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X_i] = \\textrm E[X]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y_j] = \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i \\leq 2,\\: 1\\leq j\\leq 4\n$$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\nE[Y] &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{8} + 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} = \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X+4Y]=2\\textrm E[X]+4\\textrm E[Y]= 2\\cdot \\dfrac{7}{2}+4\\cdot \\dfrac{13}{4} =20\n$$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186171875 1074 "Let $X_i$ be the $i$th score obtained from the fair die, and let $Y_j$ be the $j$th score obtained from the unfair die. Also, let $X$ and $Y$ represent the scores obtained from a typical throw of the fair and unfair die, respectively.\n\nThen, we need to calculate \n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\left[\n\\sum_{i=1}^2 X_i + \\sum_{j=1}^4 Y_j\n\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{X}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}{Y}_{j}] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}\\text{E}[{X}_{i}]+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}\\text{E}[{Y}_{j}] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[X]+4\\text{E}[Y],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the last equality above is true because $\\textrm E[X_i] = \\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[Y_j] = \\textrm E[Y]$ for all $1\\leq i \\leq 2,\\: 1\\leq j\\leq 4.$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X+4Y]=2\\textrm E[X]+4\\textrm E[Y]= 2\\cdot \\dfrac{7}{2}+4\\cdot \\dfrac{13}{4} =20.\n$$" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186172067 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432058 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186172067 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432059 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186172068 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432060 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186172068 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432062 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186172069 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432057 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186172069 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432061 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186172143 1050 420.0 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186173183 1064 "We can use the following property to simplify expressions involving expected values:\n\n> For any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b$, we have\n> $\\textrm E[aX+bY] = a \\textrm E[X] + b \\textrm E[Y]$.\n\nIn other words, the expected value of a sum of scaled random variables equals the sum of the scaled expected values.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X+5Y] = 2 \\textrm E[X] + 5 \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nNote that the property also works with subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X-5Y] = 2 \\textrm E[X] - 5 \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe can extend this idea to any number of random variables. For example, for any random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$, and real constants $a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[a_1X_1+a_2X_2+\\cdots+ a_nX_n]= a_1\\textrm E[X_1]+a_2\\textrm E[X_2]+\\cdots +a_n\\textrm{E}[X_n]\n$$\n\nor more concisely,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i X_i\\right] =\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\textrm E[X_i]\n$$\n\nNote that the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$, are *not* required to be independent." 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186173183 1064 "We can use the following property to simplify expressions involving expected values:\n\nFor any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[aX+bY] = a \\textrm E[X] + b \\textrm E[Y].\n$$\n\nIn other words, the expected value of a sum of scaled random variables equals the sum of the scaled expected values.\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X+5Y] = 2 \\textrm E[X] + 5 \\textrm E[Y].\n$$\n\nNote that the property also works with subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X-5Y] = 2 \\textrm E[X] - 5 \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe can extend this idea to any number of random variables. For example, for any random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ and real constants $a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[a_1X_1+a_2X_2+\\cdots+ a_nX_n]= a_1\\textrm E[X_1]+a_2\\textrm E[X_2]+\\cdots +a_n\\textrm{E}[X_n],\n$$\n\nor more concisely,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i X_i\\right] =\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i \\textrm E[X_i].\n$$\n\nNote that the random variables  $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ are *not* required to be independent." 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186173187 1064 "The expected value of the product of two *independent* random variables is equal to the product of the expected values. So, if the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X\\cdot Y] = \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nFor example, if we know that $X$ are $Y$ are independent, and that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X]=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y]=3\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X \\cdot Y] &= E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen working with the expected value of a product of random variables, it's important to check that the variables are independent. The formula above is *not* true if $X$ and $Y$ are not independent.\n\nMore generally, if the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are *mutually* independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X_1\\cdot X_2 \\cdots X_n]=\\textrm E[X_1]\\cdot \\textrm E[X_2]\\cdot\\ldots\\cdot \\textrm E[X_n]\n$$\n\nor more concisely,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[ \\prod_{i=1}^n X_i\\right] = \\prod_{i=1}^n \\textrm E[X_i]\n$$\n\nwhere $\\prod$ is the product operator." 13194139534671 17592186045417 false] [17592186173187 1064 "The expected value of the product of two *independent* random variables is equal to the product of the expected values. So, if the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X\\cdot Y] = \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y].\n$$\n\nFor example, if we know that $X$ are $Y$ are independent, and that $\\textrm E[X]=2$ and $\\textrm E[Y]=3$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\cdot Y] & =\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen working with the expected value of a product of random variables, it's important to check that the variables are independent. The formula above is *not* true if $X$ and $Y$ are not independent.\n\nMore generally, if the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are *mutually* independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X_1\\cdot X_2 \\cdots  X_n]=\\textrm E[X_1]\\cdot \\textrm E[X_2]\\cdot\\ldots\\cdot \\textrm E[X_n],\n$$\n\nor more concisely,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[ \\prod_{i=1}^n X_i\\right] = \\prod_{i=1}^n \\textrm E[X_i],\n$$\n\nwhere $\\prod$ is the product operator." 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432021 1080 "1.7" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432021 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432021 1083 #uuid "07711760-4d84-5bf1-aeae-3f76e11395e1" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432022 1080 "5.6" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432022 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432022 1083 #uuid "0bc88e36-dea8-5f2b-918d-a47448bd8357" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432023 1080 "Impossible to determine from the given information." 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432023 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432023 1083 #uuid "162c5f57-a9f7-53b8-b1b6-a0fc170863c8" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432024 1080 "6" 13194139534671 17592186045417 true] [17592186432024 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139